 Evaluate each anchor independently and then evaluate the combined image for perspective, occlusion, contact, shadow, reflection, material, style, and atmospheric coherence. A visually attractive composite fails when a protected asset loses its identity, design, exact markings, or required material behavior.
 
+After a local prop, identity, light, or focus edit, compare the candidate with the immutable original at matched full-frame scale and relevant crops. Check that the repair did not unexpectedly shift crop or relative scale, sharpen a deliberately soft background, brighten a deliberately dark face, repaint skin microtexture or mechanical topology, or alter the source's lighting relationship. A focus-only change does not authorize a new viewpoint; a lighting-only change does not authorize new anatomy or material relief. If perspective itself is wrong, consider a separately approved recomposition or controlled rebuild rather than hiding it with blur. Without reliable depth or masks, describe generative relighting and blur as uncertain, not physically exact.
